WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.408
Lukas: Vertical scaling adds CPU and memory. It may not lift storage I/O limits.

2
00:00:06.408 --> 00:00:12.023
Raj: It could buy us time for peaks. But our disk queues need measurement.

3
00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:18.143
Anika: I favor it as a fallback. Maybe query gains won't cover campaign load.

4
00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:24.958
Priya: Just to confirm, not approved execution, right? We still need a maintenance window.

5
00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:32.686
Hannah: And a tested rollback trigger too. Instance changes can require restart or switchover.

6
00:00:32.686 --> 00:00:40.006
Raj: Storage headroom still needs separate expansion. More memory won't store another year's data.

7
00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:46.029
Lukas: The next size has a finite ceiling. We shouldn't promise unlimited runway here.

8
00:00:46.029 --> 00:00:52.173
Priya: Okay, model one step and its cost. Compare it with the sharding option.

9
00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:59.636
Hannah: Include replica sizing and standby readiness. A writer upgrade isn't the whole fleet.

10
00:00:59.636 --> 00:01:06.380
Anika: So, run the larger-instance load test. Keep it reversible before changing production.

